Question 1: Meaning of --global in git config
The `--global` flag sets user configuration options across all Git repositories on the system. Omitting it applies the configuration only to the current repository[cite: 10].
Question 2: Contents of .git revealed by ls -a
Question 3: Approximate untracked file count
When `.venv/` is untracked, it contains hundreds or thousands of internal Python environment files[cite: 10].
Question 4: Difference in git status after creating .gitignore
Before `.gitignore`, `git status` listed all individual untracked files inside `.venv/`[cite: 5, 10]. After adding `.venv/` to `.gitignore`, `git status` no longer lists any files inside that folder[cite: 5, 10].
Question 5: Why .gitignore itself should be committed
The `.gitignore` file should be committed so that the ignore rules are shared across all collaborators and clones of the repository[cite: 5, 10].
Question 6: Movement of README.md across sections in git status
`README.md` moved from "Untracked files" to "Changes to be committed" (staged) when `git add` was run[cite: 5, 6, 10]. In the three-places model, it moved from the Working Directory to the Staging Area (Index)[cite: 5, 10].
Question 7: Output produced by git commit
Git printed the commit hash, branch name (`main`), root-commit notice, and summary of changes ("3 files changed, 57 insertions(+)")[cite: 3, 5, 10]. It reported 3 files changed[cite: 3, 5, 10].
Question 8: Name and purpose of first seven characters of commit hash
The first seven characters are called the **short commit SHA** (or commit hash)[cite: 6, 10]. They uniquely identify a specific commit snapshot in the repository history[cite: 6, 10].
Question 9: The 4 steps of the repetitive Git loop
The four steps of the Git cycle are: Edit (modify files), Stage (`git add`), Commit (`git commit`), and Push (`git push`)[cite: 6, 10].
Question 10: Special purpose and placement of README.md
Question 11: How gh auth login eliminates future password prompts
`gh auth login` authenticates via OAuth token stored locally in your system credential helper, allowing Git to authenticate automatically without requiring password entry on push[cite: 8, 10].
Question 12: File rendered on GitHub's front page
`README.md` is rendered on the front page because GitHub recognizes standard markdown documentation files by default at the repository root[cite: 8, 10].
Question 13: Effect of git push on local history
Running `git push` updates the remote branch pointer on GitHub to match the local branch, but it does not modify or alter local commit history[cite: 8, 10].
Question 14: Distinction between private repository vs. no repository
A private repository exists on GitHub's servers with full history accessible only to authorized accounts/collaborators[cite: 8, 9, 10]. If a repository does not exist, no code or history is stored or hosted on GitHub[cite: 8, 9, 10].

